1
00:00:09,622 --> 00:00:14,266
All right, let's jump into
an application protocol, HTTP.

2
00:00:15,844 --> 00:00:20,230
So when you're visiting YouTube or
any other service,

3
00:00:22,436 --> 00:00:26,750
We need an application level protocol.

4
00:00:26,750 --> 00:00:31,765
And so the transport provides
the ability to communicate,

5
00:00:31,765 --> 00:00:38,264
but the application layer tells us how
do we actually structure that data?

6
00:00:38,264 --> 00:00:40,462
How should these processes communicate?

7
00:00:40,462 --> 00:00:44,553
How should I be able to tell YouTube,
I want to watch this video?

8
00:00:44,553 --> 00:00:48,890
Or at a different time here,
I want to upload this video?

9
00:00:48,890 --> 00:00:54,300
And then how do these processes
encode the data such that the two

10
00:00:54,300 --> 00:00:59,808
parts are effectively able to
communicate with one another?

11
00:00:59,808 --> 00:01:05,870
And so this is the application layer or
application level protocol.

12
00:01:05,870 --> 00:01:13,310
And so this application layer
protocol defines these three things.

13
00:01:13,310 --> 00:01:17,857
It defines the message syntax,
that is, what fields,

14
00:01:17,857 --> 00:01:22,192
how fields are delineated,
and things like that.

15
00:01:22,192 --> 00:01:26,382
It talks about the types of messages,
exchanged,

16
00:01:26,382 --> 00:01:30,664
such as is this a request and
response type system?

17
00:01:30,664 --> 00:01:33,350
Is this streaming?

18
00:01:33,350 --> 00:01:35,580
How do the message exchange work?

19
00:01:35,580 --> 00:01:38,114
And then the message semantics.

20
00:01:38,114 --> 00:01:42,083
That is,
the meaning of information in the fields.

21
00:01:43,530 --> 00:01:48,912
So looking at the message syntax,
the requirement here is that the receiver

22
00:01:48,912 --> 00:01:54,670
needs to be able to extract the same
message as what the transmitter sent.

23
00:01:54,670 --> 00:01:58,517
And there's many different
considerations for

24
00:01:58,517 --> 00:02:03,466
how we might actually go about
this based on different goals.

25
00:02:03,466 --> 00:02:06,880
We might have a goal of debuggability, or

26
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:12,728
maybe our main concern is bandwidth or
how much processing it takes.

27
00:02:12,728 --> 00:02:15,807
And so,
as you can see in this illustration,

28
00:02:15,807 --> 00:02:21,076
we take this application data,
put it through some presentation encoding,

29
00:02:21,076 --> 00:02:24,190
which puts it into a sequence of messages.

30
00:02:24,190 --> 00:02:29,850
And at the receiver side,
it goes through a presentation decoding.

31
00:02:29,850 --> 00:02:37,338
So next we're going to go over a taxonomy
of this presentation covering data types,

32
00:02:37,338 --> 00:02:41,690
conversion strategy, and tagging approach.

33
00:02:43,470 --> 00:02:45,452
So, data types.

34
00:02:45,452 --> 00:02:50,399
So if you've programmed, you know
that there's different types of data

35
00:02:50,399 --> 00:02:54,943
that we use in our programs, but
these are also the types of things,

36
00:02:54,943 --> 00:02:58,650
the information that we want
to be able to exchange.

37
00:02:58,650 --> 00:03:03,623
So there's going to be base
types such as integers.

38
00:03:03,623 --> 00:03:11,301
So the concern here is how many
bits does an integer actually use?

39
00:03:11,301 --> 00:03:14,904
Is it 16 bits, 32, 64?

40
00:03:14,904 --> 00:03:16,853
Define what is an integer.

41
00:03:16,853 --> 00:03:21,346
And then it's also important
to define the order of bytes.

42
00:03:21,346 --> 00:03:26,754
So you may have heard of the terms
big-endian and little-endian.

43
00:03:26,754 --> 00:03:34,128
And this is an illustration of the same
number represented in two different ways.

44
00:03:34,128 --> 00:03:38,603
And that's more associated
with computer architecture.

45
00:03:38,603 --> 00:03:42,713
But if you have two machines
that are communicating,

46
00:03:42,713 --> 00:03:47,498
one may be using big-endian,
one may be using little-endian.

47
00:03:47,498 --> 00:03:52,273
So we need to be able to
how do they communicate.

48
00:03:52,273 --> 00:03:57,479
The next major data type
are what we call flat types.

49
00:03:57,479 --> 00:04:02,656
And these are arrays or structures.

50
00:04:02,656 --> 00:04:10,258
So the concern here would be things
like the padding and the length.

51
00:04:10,258 --> 00:04:12,394
How long are these?

52
00:04:12,394 --> 00:04:15,788
How do we know how many
elements are in array?

53
00:04:15,788 --> 00:04:20,156
If there's a structure that has,
so typically,

54
00:04:20,156 --> 00:04:25,668
if we create a structure in C,
the compiler might put some extra

55
00:04:25,668 --> 00:04:31,596
padding to make it align to byte
boundaries that are more fitting for

56
00:04:31,596 --> 00:04:35,652
the machine that it's
going to be running on.

57
00:04:35,652 --> 00:04:40,394
But again, if we have different machines,
those decisions might be different,

58
00:04:40,394 --> 00:04:44,390
so we need to have these things
defined as part of the protocol.

59
00:04:44,390 --> 00:04:46,871
And then lastly, complex types.

60
00:04:46,871 --> 00:04:53,651
So think of these as things like trees or
linked lists, maybe they have pointers.

61
00:04:53,651 --> 00:04:59,237
And the challenge here, or
the concern is that you must serialize or

62
00:04:59,237 --> 00:05:04,725
flatten these representations such
that you can transmit them and

63
00:05:04,725 --> 00:05:09,042
share them across the medium,
across the network.

64
00:05:10,430 --> 00:05:16,270
So the next part of the taxonomy
is the conversion strategy.

65
00:05:16,270 --> 00:05:21,375
And so this is once we've determined
on the representation and

66
00:05:21,375 --> 00:05:24,938
all those definition of data types, well,

67
00:05:24,938 --> 00:05:30,068
whose responsibility,
how do we actually transmit in this?

68
00:05:30,068 --> 00:05:35,736
Do we use, in the first case,
a canonical intermediate form?

69
00:05:35,736 --> 00:05:41,531
So this is where the sender
does all its computation

70
00:05:41,531 --> 00:05:48,674
in its own internal representation,
then before it sends it,

71
00:05:48,674 --> 00:05:54,730
it gets converted into
some agreed upon format.

72
00:05:54,730 --> 00:05:59,690
Then when it gets to the receiver,
the receiver then converts that agreed

73
00:05:59,690 --> 00:06:03,550
upon format into its own
internal representation.

74
00:06:03,550 --> 00:06:07,814
So there's this conversion that's
happening both at the send side and

75
00:06:07,814 --> 00:06:09,890
at the receive side.

76
00:06:09,890 --> 00:06:13,662
And there's this agreed upon format, and

77
00:06:13,662 --> 00:06:17,550
that's the format that gets transmitted.

78
00:06:18,930 --> 00:06:23,856
The other way that you might do
this as part of your conversion

79
00:06:23,856 --> 00:06:28,470
strategy is what's called
receiver makes right.

80
00:06:28,470 --> 00:06:35,369
And so here the sender transmits the data
in its internal representation and

81
00:06:35,369 --> 00:06:42,034
then as part of that includes
information about that representation.

82
00:06:42,034 --> 00:06:47,682
So that's enough information, so now
the receiver can convert from the sender's

83
00:06:47,682 --> 00:06:53,710
representation to its own internal
representation, if that's even needed.

84
00:06:53,710 --> 00:07:00,939
And so this has the benefit of
reducing the number of conversions,

85
00:07:00,939 --> 00:07:07,890
but it also makes that conversion
a little more complex.

86
00:07:07,890 --> 00:07:10,153
But where this really works,

87
00:07:10,153 --> 00:07:15,830
is it works well if the assumption
is a homogeneous infrastructure.

88
00:07:15,830 --> 00:07:18,808
If it's not the case that machine A and

89
00:07:18,808 --> 00:07:23,806
B that are talking, one is big-endian,
one is little-endian.

90
00:07:23,806 --> 00:07:29,190
Well, why do we even need to make
conversion to handle that case?

91
00:07:29,190 --> 00:07:34,918
So if there's homogeneity, well,
then let's just have the send or

92
00:07:34,918 --> 00:07:38,910
send in its own internal representation.

93
00:07:38,910 --> 00:07:44,650
And the assumption is the receiver
has the same internal representation.

94
00:07:46,110 --> 00:07:52,294
And then the final piece of this
taxonomy is the tagging approach.

95
00:07:52,294 --> 00:07:57,343
So we can see one approach
is to go with untagged and

96
00:07:57,343 --> 00:08:01,950
that's where you agree on the type.

97
00:08:01,950 --> 00:08:04,888
Length and location of data.

98
00:08:04,888 --> 00:08:09,802
So we've seen these in a lot
of the kind of protocols

99
00:08:09,802 --> 00:08:15,230
like TCP where it had this
agreed upon header format.

100
00:08:15,230 --> 00:08:21,082
And here it said bits 0
through 16 are this field and

101
00:08:21,082 --> 00:08:28,192
so forth and it's of this type and
it's all that information.

102
00:08:28,192 --> 00:08:30,830
So that's the untagged approach.

103
00:08:30,830 --> 00:08:35,768
And then the tagged approach
would be where you include

104
00:08:35,768 --> 00:08:39,416
in the message some tags about the data,

105
00:08:39,416 --> 00:08:44,228
like the type of the data and
the length of the data.

106
00:08:44,228 --> 00:08:47,681
And so we'll see this with
some examples like JSON.

107
00:08:50,770 --> 00:08:56,006
So now with that taxonomy of the syntax,

108
00:08:56,006 --> 00:09:02,166
now it's about the semantics
of the message and

109
00:09:02,166 --> 00:09:07,094
we'll look at that through the lens of

110
00:09:07,094 --> 00:09:11,290
an example protocol of HTTP.

111
00:09:11,290 --> 00:09:17,999
So this is widely used, it's the web's
application-layer protocol and

112
00:09:17,999 --> 00:09:24,422
it's about a client in the server
being able to exchange web objects.

113
00:09:24,422 --> 00:09:28,518
So web objects can be a BLOB of HTML text,

114
00:09:28,518 --> 00:09:33,710
it could be a JPEG image,
it could be an audio file.

115
00:09:35,010 --> 00:09:40,921
And the protocol is a request and
response of request

116
00:09:40,921 --> 00:09:47,236
between the client which
are commonly like browsers and

117
00:09:47,236 --> 00:09:54,730
getting a response from a server
that has some information.

118
00:09:54,730 --> 00:09:57,795
And these servers are all stateless,

119
00:09:57,795 --> 00:10:02,790
they don't retain information
about past client requests.

120
00:10:04,250 --> 00:10:08,964
All right, so you've seen
kind of URLs that look like this

121
00:10:08,964 --> 00:10:13,174
down at the bottom and
just breaking this apart.

122
00:10:13,174 --> 00:10:17,950
That first part,
the HTTP part is the protocol.

123
00:10:17,950 --> 00:10:24,190
The next part,
the www.example.com is the hostname and

124
00:10:24,190 --> 00:10:29,550
the rest is some path that's
used as part of HTTP.

125
00:10:31,830 --> 00:10:36,547
So the message format looks like this.

126
00:10:36,547 --> 00:10:40,166
So it's at the highest level, very simple.

127
00:10:40,166 --> 00:10:45,093
You have some start line and
then a carriage return line feed,

128
00:10:45,093 --> 00:10:49,639
a message header and then a new line,
then a blank line and

129
00:10:49,639 --> 00:10:52,636
then the message body, all right?

130
00:10:52,636 --> 00:10:55,527
And so on the request side,

131
00:10:55,527 --> 00:11:00,432
that start line would
have the request type and

132
00:11:00,432 --> 00:11:07,070
then some extra information
such as a path and a version.

133
00:11:07,070 --> 00:11:14,213
And then the message header would
then include various things

134
00:11:14,213 --> 00:11:19,576
that are more information
about the request.

135
00:11:19,576 --> 00:11:23,797
So we're not going to get into
what the message header and

136
00:11:23,797 --> 00:11:29,566
all those different fields for HTTP,
but this is the general structure.

137
00:11:29,566 --> 00:11:36,477
So the response to that request,
we'll see it's the same format.

138
00:11:36,477 --> 00:11:39,191
It has that start line and

139
00:11:39,191 --> 00:11:44,103
here it's the version,
some status code and

140
00:11:44,103 --> 00:11:49,606
then a bunch of information
as part of the header.

141
00:11:49,606 --> 00:11:54,332
An important part of this one
is this last one that says

142
00:11:54,332 --> 00:11:59,796
the Content-Type and
it's saying that it's text/html and

143
00:11:59,796 --> 00:12:04,647
this is how it's encoding
the characters as 8-bit.

144
00:12:04,647 --> 00:12:05,981
All right, and

145
00:12:05,981 --> 00:12:11,731
then you would see the message body
contains all of that HTML text.

146
00:12:14,230 --> 00:12:18,731
So the HTTP message types
that we saw in the request,

147
00:12:18,731 --> 00:12:23,592
they can include all of these
that are kind of shown here.

148
00:12:23,592 --> 00:12:29,090
But the kind of key ones to
know about are the GET and

149
00:12:29,090 --> 00:12:37,287
this is where you're retrieving some
web object identified in the URL.

150
00:12:37,287 --> 00:12:41,469
Or POST where you're sending or PUT,

151
00:12:41,469 --> 00:12:46,968
you're sending some
information to the server.

152
00:12:46,968 --> 00:12:52,424
So the GET is getting from and the POST or
PUT is sending something to the server.

153
00:12:55,272 --> 00:13:00,652
Now, diving into some of
the data format examples,

154
00:13:00,652 --> 00:13:08,379
we saw those tags in the message
header like Content-Type, text/html.

155
00:13:08,379 --> 00:13:15,994
So HTML is one example of data format,
stands for Hypertext Markup Language.

156
00:13:15,994 --> 00:13:22,450
It's really good for describing
the appearance of a web page, for example.

157
00:13:22,450 --> 00:13:27,770
And we're not going to
really cover that but

158
00:13:27,770 --> 00:13:33,097
you've seen probably examples of HTML.

159
00:13:33,097 --> 00:13:37,289
On the right hand side there's
something called JSON for

160
00:13:37,289 --> 00:13:40,090
JavaScript Object Notation.

161
00:13:40,090 --> 00:13:45,275
And this is something that's good for
passing information,

162
00:13:45,275 --> 00:13:50,890
widely used in APIs or
Application Programming Interfaces.

163
00:13:50,890 --> 00:13:57,270
And here you would see the Content-Type
specified as application/json.

164
00:13:57,270 --> 00:14:00,451
So since this might be
a little less familiar,

165
00:14:00,451 --> 00:14:05,410
we're going to dive into this
a little bit to show what this is.

166
00:14:05,410 --> 00:14:12,816
So as a quick overview in JSON, all of
the data is in these value pairs and

167
00:14:12,816 --> 00:14:19,234
name value pairs where you have this key,
which is a string and

168
00:14:19,234 --> 00:14:23,801
then the value which is any type,
a string,

169
00:14:23,801 --> 00:14:29,250
a number, an object,
an array, a boolean, null.

170
00:14:31,210 --> 00:14:37,570
Curly braces hold objects which
are these lists of name value pairs.

171
00:14:37,570 --> 00:14:44,190
And then square brackets hold
arrays which are lists of values.

172
00:14:44,190 --> 00:14:51,580
All right, so here's an example and we'll
go through kind of line by line of this.

173
00:14:53,070 --> 00:15:00,560
All right, so this is an example but
we're going to use a different example.

174
00:15:01,810 --> 00:15:06,760
We're going to use this example
of top quarterbacks and

175
00:15:06,760 --> 00:15:10,170
you could tell I'm an Eagles fan, so

176
00:15:10,170 --> 00:15:15,486
I put Jalen Hurts as the first
ranked best quarterback.

177
00:15:15,486 --> 00:15:18,157
And then Daniel Jones of the Giants and

178
00:15:18,157 --> 00:15:22,424
Dak Prescott of the Cowboys
would be the second and third.

179
00:15:22,424 --> 00:15:27,930
And then the Washington team,
I don't even rank their quarterback.

180
00:15:27,930 --> 00:15:31,414
So this is just me having fun,
this is the source of this, this is Eric.

181
00:15:31,414 --> 00:15:36,498
So if this was the information we wanted
to send, this would be a JSON BLOB.

182
00:15:36,498 --> 00:15:39,470
So this is why we're
going to go line by line.

183
00:15:39,470 --> 00:15:46,544
So the top level object name-value
pairs are, there's two of them.

184
00:15:46,544 --> 00:15:53,040
The first one is this TopQBs,
that's the name or the key.

185
00:15:53,040 --> 00:15:58,508
And then the value is this
array shown in green.

186
00:15:58,508 --> 00:16:06,184
The second name-value pair In this
object is the source is the name or

187
00:16:06,184 --> 00:16:11,260
the key and then Eric as the value and
that's a string.

188
00:16:12,480 --> 00:16:16,637
Now, if we go into looking
inside this array,

189
00:16:16,637 --> 00:16:24,720
we can see there's three elements in the
array, so there's three array elements.

190
00:16:24,720 --> 00:16:31,880
And this first array element is
an object and it's highlighted.

191
00:16:31,880 --> 00:16:37,147
And then within that object we could
see that it's now its own object,

192
00:16:37,147 --> 00:16:40,620
which has its own list of key value pairs.

193
00:16:40,620 --> 00:16:45,412
So this is the first key or name rank.

194
00:16:45,412 --> 00:16:50,620
And then the value would be the integer or
number one.

195
00:16:50,620 --> 00:16:56,800
And then we have another name or
key which is player.

196
00:16:56,800 --> 00:17:00,590
And then the value for that is an object.

197
00:17:01,920 --> 00:17:06,000
All right, so then we have
three of those element objects.

198
00:17:08,340 --> 00:17:13,633
So you can go play with
JSON once it kind of

199
00:17:13,633 --> 00:17:19,084
makes sense, real easy to understand and

200
00:17:19,084 --> 00:17:23,460
it conveys information in APIs.

201
00:17:24,840 --> 00:17:30,564
Now, you might have seen that
there's different versions of http.

202
00:17:30,564 --> 00:17:36,556
So just to give a quick kind of
highlight of what each version,

203
00:17:36,556 --> 00:17:42,152
the main innovation or
addition in each is in this table.

204
00:17:42,152 --> 00:17:46,594
But first let's look at what
an http request actually is.

205
00:17:46,594 --> 00:17:51,359
So you have some client with
a web browser, for example, and

206
00:17:51,359 --> 00:17:54,392
it's initiating a TCP connection.

207
00:17:54,392 --> 00:17:57,628
So it needs to do an http request.

208
00:17:57,628 --> 00:18:01,838
But first that's running over TCP, so
it makes the connection request, and

209
00:18:01,838 --> 00:18:04,580
we saw the three way handshake.

210
00:18:04,580 --> 00:18:09,910
And so that has round trip times,
transmission times.

211
00:18:12,040 --> 00:18:18,014
Then it's now time to make the request and

212
00:18:18,014 --> 00:18:21,260
transmit the file.

213
00:18:21,260 --> 00:18:25,444
And so if we add on the time to
transmit the file, great, and

214
00:18:25,444 --> 00:18:27,980
then we get the file received.

215
00:18:27,980 --> 00:18:32,413
So in http 1.0, any web object that you're

216
00:18:32,413 --> 00:18:37,310
requesting would go through
that entire process.

217
00:18:40,080 --> 00:18:43,170
So the first web object,
you would do that.

218
00:18:43,170 --> 00:18:47,292
Then a second web object, let's say
there's two images you wanted to get from

219
00:18:47,292 --> 00:18:50,180
the server, you would do the same thing.

220
00:18:50,180 --> 00:18:56,420
So version 1.1, added the notion
of persistent connections.

221
00:18:56,420 --> 00:19:01,354
That just meant I don't
close the TCP connection

222
00:19:01,354 --> 00:19:05,213
once I've gotten that first object.

223
00:19:05,213 --> 00:19:09,216
I can make a second request
on the same TCP connection.

224
00:19:09,216 --> 00:19:16,884
So this saves me the time for
doing this TCP three way handshake.

225
00:19:16,884 --> 00:19:23,831
And it also allows us to not have
to overcome that slow start or

226
00:19:23,831 --> 00:19:29,281
that kind of initial
probing of the bandwidth or

227
00:19:29,281 --> 00:19:34,776
the congestion levels
inside of the network.

228
00:19:34,776 --> 00:19:37,441
The more requests you put over this, well,

229
00:19:37,441 --> 00:19:40,770
it's finding what that
congestion window should be.

230
00:19:41,780 --> 00:19:45,740
So that's this 1.1 and then in 2.0,

231
00:19:45,740 --> 00:19:49,760
this allowed you to multiplex requests.

232
00:19:49,760 --> 00:19:55,104
So you didn't have to do a request and
then wait for a response.

233
00:19:55,104 --> 00:19:58,448
Then do another request and
then wait for a response.

234
00:19:58,448 --> 00:20:01,475
You can request five, six,

235
00:20:01,475 --> 00:20:06,280
whatever number of
objects all in parallel.

236
00:20:07,420 --> 00:20:11,671
And then finally the most recent 3.0,

237
00:20:11,671 --> 00:20:16,286
it introduced this ability
to use a different

238
00:20:16,286 --> 00:20:20,300
protocol called QUIC instead of TCP.

239
00:20:21,520 --> 00:20:27,020
So we didn't go over QUIC
in the transport layer.

240
00:20:28,400 --> 00:20:33,305
But it's certainly something
that if you find this

241
00:20:33,305 --> 00:20:38,450
topic interesting,
something worth looking into.

242
00:20:38,450 --> 00:20:43,456
All right, so that covers http and
how it works.

243
00:20:43,456 --> 00:20:47,990
And next time we're going to discuss QRPC.